  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Monday (Labor Day): A large closed low will be \nthe main player in the weather forecast through the next several\ndays, all the way through Labor Day. A large closed low just \noff Vancouver Island today will drop southeast to just off the \nOregon coast on Thursday. A stronger mid level wave associated \nwith this low will rotate northeast across the region this \nevening into Thursday morning bringing widespread beneficial \nrain showers. The highest rain totals will occur over NE \nWashington into the ID Panhandle as well as the Cascade crest \nwith added low level upslope flow. Rain totals for most areas \nrange from a tenth to quarter inch, with less than a tenth the \nWenatchee area into the western basin. Gusty winds ahead of this\nband of showers will lead to the potential for blowing dust \nwith southerly winds gusting to 25-35 MPH. Following this band \nof showers a mid level dry slot swings through the region on \nThursday resulting in showers mainly being confined to the \nCascades, as well as far SE Washington into the Central \nPanhandle Mountains. \n\nFrom Friday through Labor Day the regional coverage of showers \nwill be on the increase as the low moves over the region. Yet \nthe models show most of the shower activity to be isolated to \nscattered in nature. There will also be a risk for isolated \nthunderstorms each afternoon and evening mainly in the mountains\nwith uncapped CAPE values ranging from 200-600 J/KG. Storm \nmotion will be slow over Central Washington so will have to \nmonitor the burn scars in the Okanogan Highlands and East Slopes\nof the Cascades for isolated downpours from storms. With the \ncloud cover and shower activity temperatures will remain below \nnormal with highs in the mid 60s to mid 70s. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday: A warming and drying trend is expected as\nthe low pulls away from the region. This will allow high\ntemperatures for next Wednesday to warm to near normal values\nwith mid 70s to low 80s. JW",
